{"questions":[],"contract":"Return one object per distinct country value present in paid rows. Each object has exactly two fields: country (the row's country value) and total (the sum of quantity × unitPrice × (1 − discount) for each paid row with that country; a null discount is 0). Count every paid row, including duplicate rows, and include every country value, including blank or whitespace-only values. Return all countries, ordered by total descending, with ties ordered by country ascending using JavaScript string comparison. Do not round totals. Return an empty array for an empty input table or when there are no paid rows.","returns":"Array<{ country: string; total: number }>","examples":[{"name":"paid_revenue_by_country","plain":"Sums discounted revenue for paid rows by country and orders totals from highest to lowest.","assumption":"","source":"test("paid_revenue_by_country", () => {
  eq(revenueByCountry([
    { id: 1, orderDate: "2024-01-01", customer: "A", email: "a@example.com", country: "Brazil", product: "P", quantity: 2, unitPrice: 10, discount: 0.25, status: "paid" },
    { id: 2, orderDate: "2024-01-02", customer: "B", email: "b@example.com", country: "Brazil", product: "Q", quantity: 1, unitPrice: 10, discount: null, status: "paid" },
    { id: 3, orderDate: "2024-01-03", customer: "C", email: "c@example.com", country: "Japan", product: "R", quantity: 1, unitPrice: 15, discount: 0, status: "paid" }
  ]), [
    { country: "Brazil", total: 25 },
    { country: "Japan", total: 15 }
  ]);
});"},{"name":"ties_use_country_order_and_keep_all_countries","plain":"Keeps every country with the same total and orders tied country names alphabetically.","assumption":"Alphabetical means ascending JavaScript string comparison.","source":"test("ties_use_country_order_and_keep_all_countries", () => {
  eq(revenueByCountry([
    { id: 1, orderDate: "2024-01-01", customer: "A", email: "a@example.com", country: "Zulu", product: "P", quantity: 1, unitPrice: 4, discount: null, status: "paid" },
    { id: 2, orderDate: "2024-01-02", customer: "B", email: "b@example.com", country: "Alpha", product: "Q", quantity: 2, unitPrice: 2, discount: 0, status: "paid" }
  ]), [
    { country: "Alpha", total: 4 },
    { country: "Zulu", total: 4 }
  ]);
});"},{"name":"blank_country_and_unrounded_total","plain":"Includes a whitespace-only country value and preserves the exact computed total without rounding.","assumption":"The question didn't say how alphabetical ordering handles mixed case or whitespace; ascending JavaScript string comparison is reasonable because it is a deterministic, locale-independent ordering.","source":"test("blank_country_and_unrounded_total", () => {
  eq(revenueByCountry([
    { id: 1, orderDate: "2024-01-01", customer: "A", email: "a@example.com", country: " \ , product: "P", quantity: 1, unitPrice: 1, discount: 0.3333, status: "paid" }
  ]), [{ country: " ", total: 0.6667 }]);
});"},{"name":"empty_table_returns_empty_array","plain":"Returns an empty array for an empty table.","assumption":"","source":"test("empty_table_returns_empty_array", () => {
  eq(revenueByCountry([]), []);
});"}],"rules":[{"name":"unpaid_rows_do_not_change_totals","plain":"Adding unpaid rows does not change the revenue result.","assumption":"The question didn't say which status labels other than paid may occur; treating every non-paid label as excluded is reasonable because the user specified paid rows only.","source":"property("unpaid_rows_do_not_change_totals", [fc.array(fc.record({ id: fc.integer(), orderDate: fc.constantFrom("2024-01-01"), customer: fc.string(), email: fc.string(), country: fc.string(), product: fc.string(), quantity: fc.integer({ min: 0, max: 20 }), unitPrice: fc.integer({ min: 0, max: 100 }), discount: fc.constantFrom(null, 0, 0.5), status: fc.constantFrom("pending", "cancelled") }))], rows => { const paid = rows.map((r, i) => ({ ...r, id: i, status: "paid" })); return JSON.stringify(revenueByCountry(rows)) === JSON.stringify(revenueByCountry(paid)); }, { numRuns: 200 });"},{"name":"duplicate_paid_rows_count_twice","plain":"Repeating a paid row adds its revenue again to that country's total.","assumption":"","source":"property("duplicate_paid_rows_count_twice", [fc.record({ id: fc.integer(), orderDate: fc.constantFrom("2024-01-01"), customer: fc.string(), email: fc.string(), country: fc.string(), product: fc.string(), quantity: fc.integer({ min: 0, max: 20 }), unitPrice: fc.integer({ min: 0, max: 100 }), discount: fc.constantFrom(null, 0, 0.5), status: fc.constantFrom("paid") })], row => { const once = revenueByCountry([row]); const twice = revenueByCountry([row, row]); return twice.length === once.length && twice.every((x, i) => x.country === once[i].country && x.total === once[i].total * 2); }, { numRuns: 200 });"}],"shows":"Passing these checks would show that the answer follows the stated revenue formula, counts paid rows as specified, groups by the original country values, includes tied and blank country values, and applies the chosen ordering and empty-result behavior on the examples and generated cases.","limits":"These checks use only small made-up tables and 200 generated cases per property. They do not inspect the 332-row table, establish that its data is accurate, or prove correctness for every possible input."}

{"questions":[],"contract":"Return every country value found on a paid row, including blank and whitespace-only values, with one result per country. Count each paid row, including duplicate rows. For each paid row, add quantity multiplied by unitPrice multiplied by (1 minus discount); a null discount means no discount. Group by the exact country string without trimming or changing its case. Return an array of objects with exactly the fields {"country": string, "total": number}, sorted by total descending and then country alphabetically ascending. Do not round totals. Include all countries even when totals tie. Return an empty array for an empty input.","returns":"{ country: string; total: number }[]","examples":[{"name":"paid_rows_grouped_and_sorted","plain":"Counts paid rows, applies discounts, groups by country, and sorts totals from highest to lowest.","assumption":"","source":"test("paid_rows_grouped_and_sorted", () => {
  const rows: Row[] = [
    { id: 1, orderDate: "2024-01-01", customer: "A", email: "a@example.com", country: "Canada", product: "P", quantity: 2, unitPrice: 10, discount: 0.25, status: "paid" },
    { id: 2, orderDate: "2024-01-02", customer: "B", email: "b@example.com", country: "Brazil", product: "Q", quantity: 1, unitPrice: 30, discount: null, status: "paid" },
    { id: 3, orderDate: "2024-01-03", customer: "C", email: "c@example.com", country: "Canada", product: "R", quantity: 1, unitPrice: 4, discount: null, status: "paid" },
    { id: 4, orderDate: "2024-01-04", customer: "D", email: "d@example.com", country: "Japan", product: "S", quantity: 1, unitPrice: 100, discount: null, status: "pending" }
  ];
  eq(revenueByCountry(rows), [{ country: "Brazil", total: 30 }, { country: "Canada", total: 19 }]);
});"},{"name":"ties_alphabetical_and_all_countries_included","plain":"Keeps every country with equal totals and orders tied totals alphabetically.","assumption":"","source":"test("ties_alphabetical_and_all_countries_included", () => {
  const rows: Row[] = [
    { id: 1, orderDate: "2024-01-01", customer: "A", email: "a@example.com", country: "Zulu", product: "P", quantity: 1, unitPrice: 5, discount: null, status: "paid" },
    { id: 2, orderDate: "2024-01-02", customer: "B", email: "b@example.com", country: "Alpha", product: "Q", quantity: 1, unitPrice: 5, discount: null, status: "paid" }
  ];
  eq(revenueByCountry(rows), [{ country: "Alpha", total: 5 }, { country: "Zulu", total: 5 }]);
});"},{"name":"empty_input_returns_empty_array","plain":"Returns an empty array when the table has no rows.","assumption":"","source":"test("empty_input_returns_empty_array", () => {
  eq(revenueByCountry([]), []);
});"},{"name":"blank_country_is_included_and_no_rounding","plain":"Includes a whitespace-only country value and preserves the exact calculated total without rounding.","assumption":"","source":"test("blank_country_is_included_and_no_rounding", () => {
  const rows: Row[] = [
    { id: 1, orderDate: "2024-01-01", customer: "A", email: "a@example.com", country: " ", product: "P", quantity: 3, unitPrice: 1.01, discount: 0.1, status: "paid" }
  ];
  eq(revenueByCountry(rows), [{ country: " ", total: 2.727 }]);
});"}],"rules":[{"name":"unpaid_rows_do_not_change_answer","plain":"Adding a row whose status is not paid does not change the revenue totals.","assumption":"","source":"property("unpaid_rows_do_not_change_answer", [fc.array(fc.record({ id: fc.integer(), orderDate: fc.constantFrom("2024-01-01"), customer: fc.string(), email: fc.string(), country: fc.string(), product: fc.string(), quantity: fc.integer({ min: 0, max: 20 }), unitPrice: fc.integer({ min: 0, max: 100 }), discount: fc.constantFrom(null, 0, 0.25, 0.5), status: fc.constantFrom("paid", "pending", "cancelled") }))], rows => {
  const unpaid = rows.map((row, index) => ({ ...row, id: row.id + index, status: "pending" }));
  return JSON.stringify(revenueByCountry(rows)) === JSON.stringify(revenueByCountry([...rows, ...unpaid]));
}, { numRuns: 200 });"},{"name":"duplicating_paid_rows_doubles_totals","plain":"Adding another copy of each paid row adds its revenue again, including for duplicate rows.","assumption":"","source":"property("duplicating_paid_rows_doubles_totals", [fc.array(fc.record({ id: fc.integer(), orderDate: fc.constantFrom("2024-01-01"), customer: fc.string(), email: fc.string(), country: fc.string(), product: fc.string(), quantity: fc.integer({ min: 0, max: 20 }), unitPrice: fc.integer({ min: 0, max: 100 }), discount: fc.constantFrom(null, 0, 0.25, 0.5), status: fc.constantFrom("paid", "pending") }))], rows => {
  const paid = rows.filter(row => row.status === "paid");
  const combined = revenueByCountry([...rows, ...paid]);
  const expected = revenueByCountry(rows).map(item => ({ country: item.country, total: item.total + item.totalForPaidPlaceholder }));
  const addedByCountry = new Map<string, number>();
  for (const row of paid) {
    const amount = row.quantity * row.unitPrice * (1 - (row.discount ?? 0));
    addedByCountry.set(row.country, (addedByCountry.get(row.country) ?? 0) + amount);
  }
  const expectedTotals = new Map<string, number>();
  for (const item of revenueByCountry(rows)) expectedTotals.set(item.country, item.total);
  for (const [country, amount] of addedByCountry) expectedTotals.set(country, (expectedTotals.get(country) ?? 0) + amount);
  const expectedSorted = [...expectedTotals].map(([country, total]) => ({ country, total })).sort((a, b) => b.total - a.total || (a.country < b.country ? -1 : a.country > b.country ? 1 : 0));
  return JSON.stringify(combined) === JSON.stringify(expectedSorted);
}, { numRuns: 200 });"},{"name":"totals_match_direct_paid_row_sum","plain":"Each returned country total equals the sum of its paid rows using quantity, unit price, and discount, with null discount treated as zero.","assumption":"","source":"matchesReference("totals_match_direct_paid_row_sum", [fc.array(fc.record({ id: fc.integer(), orderDate: fc.constantFrom("2024-01-01"), customer: fc.string(), email: fc.string(), country: fc.string(), product: fc.string(), quantity: fc.integer({ min: 0, max: 20 }), unitPrice: fc.integer({ min: 0, max: 100 }), discount: fc.constantFrom(null, 0, 0.25, 0.5), status: fc.constantFrom("paid", "pending", "cancelled") }))], rows => {
  const totals = new Map<string, number>();
  for (const row of rows) if (row.status === "paid") totals.set(row.country, (totals.get(row.country) ?? 0) + row.quantity * row.unitPrice * (1 - (row.discount ?? 0)));
  return [...totals].map(([country, total]) => ({ country, total })).sort((a, b) => b.total - a.total || (a.country < b.country ? -1 : a.country > b.country ? 1 : 0));
}, { numRuns: 200 });"}],"shows":"Passing these checks would show that the function follows the stated paid-row revenue formula, groups rows by exact country value, counts duplicates, includes blank country values, returns exact unrounded totals, sorts by descending total with alphabetical ties, keeps all countries, and returns an empty array for empty input on the tested examples and generated tables.","limits":"The checks use four small made-up tables and 200 generated tables per rule; they do not inspect the actual 332-row table, every possible input, unusual numeric values, or behavior outside the stated row type."}
